          },
          {
            "id": 2835797,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "Scientists believe that a majority of the Earth's bird population migrate in some fashion or other. Some travel seasonally for relatively short distances, such as birds that move from their winter habitats in lowlands to mountaintops for the summers. Others, like the Arctic tern, travel more than 25,000 miles seasonally between the Northern and southern poles. Bird migration has been studied over many centuries through a variety of observations, but until relatively recently, where birds went to in the winter was considered something of a mystery. The lack of modern science and technology led to many theories that we now recognise as error filled and even somewhat amusing.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2835798,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Scientists believe that a majority of the Earth's bird population migrate in some fashion or other.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 77.645,
                  "from": 70.604996,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2835815,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Some travel seasonally for relatively short distances, such as birds that move from their winter habitats in lowlands to mountaintops for the summers.",
